        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Sikhote-Alin is a group IIAB iron meteorite that fell on 12 February 1947 at about 10:30 a.m. in the Sikhote-Alin Mountains of the Russian Far East. A metallic asteroid of about 70 to 100 tonnes, a fragment of the core of a destroyed parent body, entered the atmosphere at over 14 km/s; it broke up at about 6 km altitude, producing a fireball brighter than the Sun, visible 300 km away, and a shower of thousands of fragments. The largest masses dug about a hundred craters, the biggest 26 metres across; about 23 tonnes have been recovered.",
            "Iron-nickel alloy (about 93% iron, 5.9% nickel, with cobalt, phosphorus and sulphur); very coarse octahedrite (or coarse hexahedrite-octahedrite) dominated by kamacite, with large schreibersite crystals (iron-nickel phosphide). Specific gravity about 7.8; strongly magnetic. There are two kinds of specimens: complete individuals that fell alone, covered in fusion crust and “regmaglypts” (thumbprints carved by the air), highly sought after; and shrapnel fragments with twisted shapes, torn apart in the explosion. Its Neumann lines (fine shock lines) appear on etching.",
            "Russia: Primorsky Krai, about 440 km north-east of Vladivostok. Expeditions of the USSR Academy of Sciences searched the strewn field (1.3 km²) for decades; a monument and a reserve mark the site. Most trade pieces were found later with metal detectors and circulate legally through specialist dealers. A 1957 Soviet stamp commemorates the fall.",
            "The fall, seen by many witnesses, was painted by the artist P. I. Medvedev, who saw it; his work served as the model for the commemorative stamp. Sikhote-Alin is said to give strength, courage and the ability to get through crises, and to connect with celestial energies. It is associated with the root chakra and the sign of Aries. Meteoritic iron prone to rust: avoid water and humidity; do not wash; brush dry; protect with a thin coat of oil; store dry; cleanse by smudging."
        ),
